% Allena Luce, Canciones populares (1921).
% Published early from the workshop on 11 November 2026: each tune names what is guessed or left out in its N: lines,
% and its workshop record (or review item) is kept for further correction.

X:1
T:Si me dan pasteles
T:If You Give Me Pasteles
C:traditional (Puerto Rico)
O:Puerto Rico
R:aguinaldo (Christmas song)
S:Allena Luce, Canciones populares (New York: Silver, Burdett, 1921), "Aguinaldo (Si me dan pasteles)", p. 3
N:A Puerto Rican aguinaldo. At Christmas, groups of singers and players go from house to house (the parranda) and the hosts give them a gift or something to eat; the song's frank hint is that the visitors want pasteles (Christmas parcels of mashed plantain and root vegetables with a filling, wrapped in banana leaves), and hot ones, because cold pasteles give people a stomach-ache. A Christmas custom with secular words. The instruments play the ritornello before the verse and between verses; the voices sing in thirds, half the class on each line; then make up a verse for the class's own party food, as the parrandas do. Guessed: the first note of the ritornello's bar 8 (read d; it could be c), the lower voice in bar 7 of the verse (G under the c), and the last bar, where Luce puts both syllables of "gen-te" under one chord. The güiro, maracas and chords of the band are ours, after the instruments documented for aguinaldos (cuatro, tres, güiro, maracas, claves, bongó); the tempo is ours.
Q:1/4=104
M:2/4
L:1/8
K:Gm
